al physical exam", "Vitamin D intoxication: Could explain hypercalcemia but lacks supporting evidence"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional weight loss", "Neurological dysfunction (trouble focusing)", "Systemic metabolic derangement (hypercalcemia + elevated ALP)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Malignancy-Associated Hypercalcemia", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.6500000000000001, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Systemic symptoms like weight loss and neurological dysfunction are atypical for antacid-induced hypercalcemia, strongly excluding this cause."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Primary Hyperparathyroidism", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.099, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Primary hyperparathyroidism typically causes hypercalciuria, making low urine sodium a moderate exclusionary finding."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Vitamin D Excess / Related Hypercalcemia", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.095, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Granulomatous disease like sarcoidosis would typically present with skin lesions, reducing support for vitamin D excess."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Reactive Hypercalcemia / Medication-Induced", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.04, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Systemic symptoms like weight loss and neurological dysfunction are atypical for antacid-induced hypercalcemia, strongly excluding this cause."]}, "B5": {